---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Which is the best internet provider in Pakistan?',
    a: 'There is no single winner, it depends on what you measure. Nayatel leads on nPerf’s 2025 barometer (best fixed performance), PakSpeed’s recent community tests, and Opensignal’s April 2026 reliability award. PTCL Flash Fiber leads on Ookla’s H1 2025 Speedtest score and SpeedGeo’s ranking. Transworld is close behind both. The honest answer: the best provider is the fastest fibre available on your street.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which provider has the fastest internet in Pakistan?',
    a: 'On published averages, Nayatel and PTCL Flash Fiber trade the top spot depending on the study. Nayatel averages 28.3 Mbps download on nPerf 2025 and 32.8 Mbps on PakSpeed’s last 30 days of tests; PTCL Flash Fiber averages 31.7 Mbps on SpeedGeo and scores highest on Ookla. Both are far ahead of DSL and mobile data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which ISP is best for gaming in Pakistan?',
    a: 'Fibre with the lowest ping, PTCL Flash Fiber averages 27 ms on SpeedGeo’s ranking, and StormFiber averages 60 ms on PakSpeed’s recent tests. Avoid DSL for gaming: its ping is higher and more variable. Whichever provider you choose, play wired, not on Wi-Fi.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which mobile network is fastest in Pakistan?',
    a: 'Zong, on the PTA’s Q2 2026 benchmarking: 18.6 Mbps average 4G download, 7.8 Mbps upload, and 96.6% of time on 4G. Jazz is close behind (17.6 Mbps) and is fastest in Islamabad, Rawalpindi, Faisalabad and Peshawar. For 5G, Jazz has the biggest network; Zong leads in several smaller cities.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is PTCL the best internet provider in Pakistan?',
    a: 'PTCL is the biggest and most widely available, and its Flash Fiber is genuinely among the fastest in the country. But PTCL’s overall averages are dragged down by its large DSL base, so "PTCL" as a whole ranks below Nayatel and Transworld on several studies. The comparison that matters is Flash Fiber versus the other fibre providers, where PTCL is right at the top.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Best Internet Provider in Pakistan', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/best-internet-provider-pakistan' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Best Internet Provider in Pakistan: Ranked on Speed Data"
  description="Best ISP in Pakistan, ranked on published data: Nayatel, PTCL Flash Fiber, Transworld and StormFiber on speed and ping, plus the best by city."
  canonical="/best-internet-provider-pakistan"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Best Internet Provider</p>
      <h1>Best Internet Provider in Pakistan</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Every "best ISP" list you’ll find online is either sponsored or based on one
        study. This one isn’t: it combines five published data sources, shows where
        they disagree, and admits the truth, the best provider is the fastest fibre
        available on your street.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">Last updated: 21 November 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>The ranking</h2>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Provider</th><th>Why it ranks here</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Nayatel</strong></td>
            <td>Best fixed performance on nPerf 2025 (score 52,257), highest PakSpeed 30-day average (32.8 Mbps), and Opensignal’s Best Home Internet + Reliability award (April 2026). Limited to Islamabad, Rawalpindi, Faisalabad, Peshawar and selected cities.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2</strong></td>
            <td><strong>PTCL Flash Fiber</strong></td>
            <td>Highest Ookla Speedtest score in Pakistan, H1 2025 (56.95), and first on SpeedGeo’s ranking (31.7 Mbps download, 27 ms ping). Available in far more places than Nayatel.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>3</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Transworld</strong></td>
            <td>Second on Ookla H1 2025 (56.71) and second on PakSpeed’s 30-day average (29.1 Mbps). Strongest in Karachi.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>4</strong></td>
            <td><strong>StormFiber</strong></td>
            <td>Third on PakSpeed’s 30-day average (26.7 Mbps) with the lowest ping of the big fibre providers on that study (60 ms). Serves Karachi, Lahore, Islamabad, Faisalabad and other major cities.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>5</strong></td>
            <td><strong>PTCL (overall)</strong></td>
            <td>The nationwide incumbent, but its averages mix fibre with older DSL, so it ranks below the pure-fibre providers (21.7 Mbps on PakSpeed, 22.4 Mbps on nPerf). The right choice where no other fibre reaches.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="small muted">
        Sources: nPerf fixed-internet barometer 2025; Ookla Speedtest Awards H1 2025;
        PakSpeed community tests (last 30 days); SpeedGeo broadband ranking (Q4 2025 –
        Q3 2026); Opensignal Fixed Broadband Experience Report, April 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>Best by use case</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fastest measured speeds:</strong> Nayatel or PTCL Flash Fiber, they trade the top spot depending on the study.</li>
        <li><strong>Widest availability:</strong> PTCL, DSL almost everywhere, Flash Fiber in many cities. No other provider comes close on reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Lowest ping (gaming):</strong> PTCL Flash Fiber (27 ms average on SpeedGeo) or StormFiber (60 ms on PakSpeed). Both beat DSL by a wide margin.</li>
        <li><strong>Most reliable:</strong> Nayatel, Opensignal’s April 2026 report gave it the Reliability Experience award (330 points).</li>
        <li><strong>Best mobile internet:</strong> Zong has the highest national 4G averages (PTA Q2 2026); Jazz is fastest in Islamabad, Rawalpindi, Faisalabad and Peshawar. For 5G, Jazz has the biggest network.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Best by city</h2>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>City</th><th>Fastest fixed provider</th><th>Fastest mobile</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Karachi</td><td>Transworld (37.4 Mbps)</td><td>Zong (18.27 Mbps)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lahore</td><td>PTCL (30.6 Mbps)</td><td>Zong (21.26 Mbps)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Islamabad</td><td>PTCL (39.1 Mbps)</td><td>Jazz (20.86 Mbps)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Faisalabad</td><td>Nayatel (34.7 Mbps)</td><td>Jazz (19.78 Mbps)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multan</td><td>No published figure</td><td>Zong (21.60 Mbps)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Peshawar</td><td>No published figure</td><td>Jazz (fastest, no figure published)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quetta</td><td>No published figure</td><td>Jazz (22.41 Mbps)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="small muted">
        Fixed figures: PakSpeed city tables, last 90 days. Mobile figures: PTA Mobile
        Network Experience Benchmarking, Q2 2026. See the full detail on our
        <a href="/cities"> city pages</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How this ranking was made</h2>
      <p>
        We combined five published sources rather than trusting one: nPerf’s 2025
        barometer (large-sample, methodical), Ookla’s H1 2025 Speedtest scores, PakSpeed’s
        community tests (recent, but self-selected), SpeedGeo’s ranking (158,631 tests),
        and Opensignal’s April 2026 experience report. Where the sources disagree, and
        they do, because samples and methods differ, we say so on the
        <a href="/statistics"> statistics page</a> and in each
        <a href="/compare"> head-to-head comparison</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things this ranking deliberately does not do: it does not rank on price
        (published prices conflict wildly and go stale fast), and it does not treat a
        national average as a promise for your street. Fibre availability varies by
        street, and your line’s real speed is what you measure, test it.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Test your connection</h2>
        <p>Rankings are averages, your line is the only number that matters.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/compare">Compare providers →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/statistics">Speed statistics →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
